#{[17592186079363 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079363 #edb/ref 1061 192027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079363 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "de017b4d-d30f-53a6-907e-76f2041922d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079363 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186079730 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079364 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079364 #edb/ref 1061 192028 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079364 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "59d284ba-ad7f-51c6-9702-393b4df65edd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079364 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078468 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079365 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079365 #edb/ref 1061 192032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079365 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "86b3cd64-20d2-5c3f-a721-8d1eb42c0252" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079365 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078469 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079366 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079366 #edb/ref 1061 192035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079366 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9a20f271-7fe8-5fe6-a484-e4fb2d2becdb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079366 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078470 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079367 #edb/ref 1061 192038 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079367 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "365e24e0-7b67-5ec0-b97d-dd778e400ad7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079367 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078471 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079505 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079506 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079505 #edb/ref 1061 653769 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079505 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "32f48700-2e28-53f4-8117-05414a51c990" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079505 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186079624 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079506 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079506 #edb/ref 1061 653770 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079506 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "03cdecc5-044c-525b-9d1a-1f3234b5c9a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079506 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078561 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079507 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079507 #edb/ref 1061 653774 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079507 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c84a2326-edc8-5f45-9916-727883af7cec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079507 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078562 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079508 #edb/ref 1061 653778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079508 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "01600498-e551-5c5b-b3e4-ea3cc665f3c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079508 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078563 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079624 #edb/ref 1064 "When simplifying expressions, we prefer not to leave a radical in the denominator. To eliminate a radical in an expression's denominator, we can use a process known as **rationalizing the denominator**.\n\nTo rationalize the denominator, we need to multiply both the numerator and denominator by the radical that we wish to remove from the denominator.\n\nFor instance, to rationalize the denominator in the expression\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x}},\n$$\n\nall we need to do is multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{x}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\cdot \\,\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{\\sqrt{x}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{(\\sqrt{x})}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{x} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the final expression no longer has a radical in the denominator. Thus, the denominator has been rationalized.\n\n**Note:** Because we multiplied the numerator and denominator by the same amount, the resulting expression $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{x}}{x}$ is equivalent to the original fraction $\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}.$ They are equivalent, even though they are written differently." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079624 #edb/ref 1065 1614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079624 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "17e8e5bd-0b03-5315-8d7a-fc97948fb0d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079624 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079720 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's think about how to graph the solutions to the following two-variable inequality:\n\n$$\ny \\gt x\n$$\n\nSince this is a two-variable inequality, we must represent its solutions in the $xy$-plane.\n\nWe start by replacing the inequality symbol $(>)$ with an equals sign $(=)$ and plot the resulting line. In this case, we get the line $y=x$.\n\n![](images/34/3497eb8d3f071794cd899c5c62a88500fbcf7b69a34d58171866a078a185bfc9.png)\n\nNotice that we have a \"*greater than*\" $(>)$ inequality. Therefore, we draw a *dashed* line to denote that points on the line are *not* solutions to our inequality.\n\nNow, since we're solving $y > x$, we need to shade all points whose $y$-value is *greater than* its $x$-value. This is given by all of the points that lie *above* our dashed line. So, let's shade these in.\n\n![](images/74/74fef6fbfabb750d09bba12b30f06bc9fd625ef824bdb3388bc66fe03a02c48c.png)\n\nAnd we're done! All of the points in the shaded region satisfy the inequality $y > x$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079720 #edb/ref 1065 8721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079720 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ce943551-fa59-5976-8896-2c757f4b230a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079720 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079721 #edb/ref 1064 "When solving two-variable inequalities, it's often helpful to test to ensure we've shaded the correct region.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's go back to the following inequality:\n\n$$\ny > x\n$$\n\nThe solution to this inequality is indicated by the shaded region below.\n\n![](images/2c/2ceb4ad142fc943e9c5a95c43ba4e3a309669f09d383540fdfcb42f8cb48bf7c.png)\n\nTo check that we've shaded the correct region, we pick a point inside the shaded region and verify that it satisfies the original inequality. Let's pick the point $(0,1)$.\n\n![](images/29/29fc8bba0c205e7aa782fed0bb3cf348d4acc92b806eef60997917ade05905d5.png)\n\nWhen we substitute the coordinates $(0,1)$ into our inequality, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}y & > x \\\\ 1 & > 0✓\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis indicates that we have shaded the correct region." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079721 #edb/ref 1065 8722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079721 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a4437a9f-4fe6-5dcf-a771-61bba9e944f1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079721 #edb/ref 1067 "Testing the Solution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079722 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's now think about how to graph the solutions to the inequality\n\n$$\ny \\lt x\n$$\n\nAs before, we start by replacing the inequality symbol $(\\lt)$ with an equals sign $(=)$ and plot the resulting line $y=x$.\n\n![](images/72/72b217c75c8ec84681212a8ab48614fb777e05e7022ca338897d16763070c816.png)\n\nNotice that we have a \"*less than*\" $(\\lt)$ inequality. Therefore, we draw a *dashed* line.\n\nNow, since we're solving $y \\lt x$, we need to shade all points whose $y$-value is *smaller than* its $x$-value. This is given by all of the points that lie *below* our dashed line.\n\n![](images/f7/f7259181cf578ae89840d42c752b9f36f84862a95dd3b03bc6e5b1aba7870e93.png)\n\nAnd we're done! All of the points in the shaded region satisfy the inequality $y \\lt x$.\n\nTo check that we've shaded the correct region, we pick a point inside the shaded region and check that it satisfies the original inequality. Let's pick the point $(1,0)$.\n\n![](images/e3/e33d6402bfcbc4fad89775c451a0048f09aee83753b790ce9c98e465b3f374e1.png)\n\nWhen we substitute the coordinates $(1,0)$ into our inequality, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}y & < x \\\\ 0 & < 1✓\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis indicates that we have shaded the correct region." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079722 #edb/ref 1065 8731 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079722 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "760096fa-2690-595f-b535-65cec94088a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079722 #edb/ref 1067 "Solving \"Strictly Less Than\" Inequalities" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079730 #edb/ref 1064 "In addition to using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} + (n - 1)d\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto compute the terms of an arithmetic sequence, we can also use it to compute the **index** of a particular term. The index of a term is simply the value of $n$ corresponding to that term.\n\nTo illustrate, suppose we're given the following arithmetic sequence:\n\n$$\n1, \\: 4, \\: 7, \\: 10, \\ \\ldots, \\: 28, \\: \\ldots\n$$\n\nIf the first term is $1$, the second term is $4$, the third term is $7$, and so on, then what term gives the value $28$? In other words, what value of $n$ gives $a_n=28$?\n\nSince $a_1=1$ and $d=3$, we can construct the formula for the $n$th term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} + (n - 1)d \\\\\n&= 1 + (n - 1)(3) \\\\\n&= 1 + 3n - 3 \\\\\n&= 3n - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can solve for the value of $n$ that gives $a_n=28$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= 3n - 2 \\\\\n28 &= 3n - 2 \\\\\n30 &= 3n \\\\\n10 &= n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $28$ is the $10$th term of the sequence." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079730 #edb/ref 1065 8915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079730 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ea5fca63-4618-5148-9355-8de0ed7707db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079730 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186080333 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186080334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186080333 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186080335 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186080333 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186080336 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186080333 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186080337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186080333 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186080338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186080333 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186080333 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 1759